\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1}{\ignorespaces Redundancy density distribution across the controlled synthetic benchmark. The per-trace redundancy density (mean $0.384$) confirms that reflective traces contain substantial pairwise step similarity, motivating the explicit redundancy penalty in the SCU objective. Without this term, audit weight can be over-allocated to repeated checks.}}{16}{figure.1}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:redundancy-density}{{1}{16}{Redundancy density distribution across the controlled synthetic benchmark. The per-trace redundancy density (mean $0.384$) confirms that reflective traces contain substantial pairwise step similarity, motivating the explicit redundancy penalty in the SCU objective. Without this term, audit weight can be over-allocated to repeated checks}{figure.1}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3}{\ignorespaces PRM800K signal comparison across ranking methods. The learned \texttt  {w\_struct} baseline and its SC-FMA Ridge preservation clearly outperform raw local utility, simple averaging, and the frozen PRM prefix-score baseline on the locked hash split. QP and Projection are downgraded on this route, consistent with the variant-level claim boundary.}}{16}{figure.3}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:prm-comparison}{{3}{16}{PRM800K signal comparison across ranking methods. The learned \texttt {w\_struct} baseline and its SC-FMA Ridge preservation clearly outperform raw local utility, simple averaging, and the frozen PRM prefix-score baseline on the locked hash split. QP and Projection are downgraded on this route, consistent with the variant-level claim boundary}{figure.3}{}}
